// Deal or Force - long-range forces on the map: how they travel, where they may stand, what
// crowding and lost ground cost them, and how the computer moves its own (LongRangeMovement.cs).
//
// The road pace is not here: a formation drives at a division's MarchProgressPerDay
// (07_movement.json), over the same terrain and roads, times the "speed" of its kind
// (common/support); inland, where neither end of a step touches the enemy, it goes by train at
// RailRedeploymentSpeed, the same railways the divisions use.
//
// Every key overrides the value compiled into LongRangeMovement.cs; a key no file mentions keeps
// its compiled value, and files in this folder are read in name order with later files winning.
{
  // By sea.
  //
  // Where there is no road - an island, an enclave, an ally over the water - the formation
  // drives to a port of its own side, ships, and lands at a port in the same sea. 450 km a day
  // is a transport with heavy equipment at about ten knots.
  "LongRangeSeaKmPerDay": 450,
  // Loading a brigade at one end and unloading it at the other is days, not hours: two at each.
  "LongRangePortDays": 2,

  // By air.
  //
  // A squadron is there in hours; its ground echelon, fuel, munitions and technicians are not,
  // and they are what a relocation waits for. 1,500 km a day puts a wing across Russia (6,000 km)
  // in four days and one more to deploy - neither instant nor weeks.
  "LongRangeAirKmPerDay": 1500,
  // One ferry leg, unarmed and with drop tanks, is about three combat radii...
  "LongRangeFerryRangeFactor": 3,
  // ...never less than a helicopter is ferried (500-700 km)...
  "LongRangeFerryMinKm": 600,
  // ...and never more than this: past it is air-to-air refuelling, which the game does not have.
  // Longer trips are flown in legs - Shift+right-click adds a waypoint.
  "LongRangeFerryMaxKm": 4000,

  // Lost ground.
  //
  // The share of strength a formation loses, once, when the province it stands in falls to the
  // enemy: what is heavy or broken and could not be got out. Less and losing a province costs
  // nothing; more and one breakthrough knocks out half a brigade. It then withdraws like any
  // other move - and with no way out it is cut off where it stands.
  "LongRangeOverrunLoss": 0.25,

  // Reach.
  //
  // Added to every formation's range: half an average province (about 65 km across). Range is
  // measured from the centre of the base province to the centre of the target, and the guns
  // fire to the edge - without it a 45 km gun line did not reach the next province.
  "LongRangeReachSlackKm": 35,

  // Basing.
  //
  // Not a limit any more but a price: a region bases this much without crowding, and past it
  // the readiness its formations recover is multiplied by capacity / used. A battery takes 1, an
  // air wing 2 and a bomber regiment 3 ("basing" in common/support). The allowance is one or two
  // formations without barracks, as before, plus one.
  "LongRangeBasingBase": 3,
  // What the old "posts per barracks" was, now here where a mod can reach it.
  "LongRangeBasingPerBarracks": 2,
  // A city is airfields, depots and rail yards to move into; counted for the leading cities its
  // owner still holds.
  "LongRangeBasingPerCity": 2,

  // Training.
  //
  // Formations one country may have in training at once - what it was, now visible in the panel
  // and changeable by a mod.
  "SupportTrainingSlots": 8,

  // The computer.
  //
  // Days between its reviews of where its formations stand. A front moves a province or two in a
  // fortnight; reviewing more often only buys more moves.
  "AiLongRangeRepositionDays": 15,
  // It puts a formation within this share of its reach of the nearest battle or front, so the
  // front can move a little and the formation still reaches.
  "AiLongRangeStandoffShare": 0.6,
  // Guns of shorter range than this stand on the front itself; everything longer-ranged stands
  // back from it, where the enemy's guns do not reach.
  "AiLongRangeFrontlineMaxRangeKm": 100
}
